(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["graphs",{"l:graphs/why-graphs-workflows-as-state-machines":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An agent loop lets the model choose every next step. A \u003cb>graph\u003c/b> makes the control flow explicit: \u003cb>nodes\u003c/b> do the work, \u003cb>edges\u003c/b> decide what runs next, and a shared \u003cb>state\u003c/b> object carries data between them. \u003cb>Graph engineering\u003c/b> is designing that state machine so plain code handles everything that must always happen, and the model decides only where judgement is genuinely needed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The agent loop is already a graph\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The minimal agent from the Agents module is a loop: call the model, run any tools it asks for, append the results, repeat until it answers without asking for a tool. Drawn as a graph it has two nodes and one decision:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>            +---------+   tool calls    +---------+\n START ---&gt; |  model  | --------------&gt; |  tools  |\n            +---------+ &lt;-------------- +---------+\n                 |          results\n                 | no tool calls\n                 v\n                END\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every decision about what happens next is made by the model at run time. That is exactly right for open-ended work such as coding or research, where nobody can list the steps in advance. It is the wrong default for a business process in which some steps are mandatory, some need a human, and the whole thing may take two days.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When a loop is not enough\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Requirement\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why a free loop struggles\u003c/th>\u003cth>What an explicit graph gives you\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Mandatory steps (verify identity before a refund)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The prompt asks for it; the model can still skip it\u003c/td>\u003ctd>There is simply no edge around the step\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Approval before an action\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Ask before refunding\" is a request, not a guarantee\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An approval node that pauses the run\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Runs that outlive a process\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The loop's state lives in memory and dies with it\u003c/td>\u003ctd>State saved after every step, resumable by id\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Parallel work\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One model call at a time\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fan-out to several nodes, fan-in to merge\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Different models or prompts per step\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One growing context serves every step\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Each node has its own prompt, model and tools\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Audit and debugging\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One long transcript\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Named steps with a state snapshot after each\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost predictability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model decides how many steps to take\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bounded paths; cycles carry counters\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>If none of these apply, keep the loop. If two or three apply, you want a graph, usually with a small agent loop inside one or two of its nodes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>State machines in a few paragraphs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A \u003cb>state machine\u003c/b> is a set of states plus rules for moving between them. LLM orchestration uses slightly different words for the same thing:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>State\u003c/b>: a typed record shared by the whole run (the user message, the order id, the draft reply, an approval flag).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Node\u003c/b>: a function that reads the state and returns an update. It can be plain code, one LLM call, a whole agent or another graph.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Edge\u003c/b>: a transition. A \u003ci>normal\u003c/i> edge always goes from A to B; a \u003ci>conditional\u003c/i> edge calls a routing function that looks at the state and names the next node.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Agent graphs allow \u003cb>cycles\u003c/b> (edges that lead back to an earlier node), unlike DAG schedulers such as Airflow or Dagster. Cycles are what let a graph express \"revise until the checker passes\" or the agent loop itself.\u003c/p>\u003cp>LangGraph runs a graph in discrete \u003cb>super-steps\u003c/b>, an idea borrowed from Google's Pregel system. In each super-step every node that was triggered runs (in parallel if there are several), their updates are merged into the state, a checkpoint can be saved, and the edges decide which nodes run in the next super-step. The run ends when no node is triggered. You will use this model in the lessons on parallel branches and checkpoints.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A worked example: a refund desk\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Message\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Classify\u003c/b>\u003csmall>LLM\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Look up order\u003c/b>\u003csmall>code\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Check policy\u003c/b>\u003csmall>code\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Approval\u003c/b>\u003csmall>human\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Issue refund\u003c/b>\u003csmall>code\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Reply\u003c/b>\u003csmall>LLM\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Classify\u003c/b>\u003csmall>question\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Retrieve\u003c/b>\u003csmall>code\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Answer\u003c/b>\u003csmall>LLM with citations\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The model appears in three of nine nodes. The policy rule \"refunds over 200 need a manager\" is enforced in code, not suggested in a prompt. The approval cannot be skipped because no edge goes around it. The run can wait at the approval step for a day and survive a redeploy, because its state is saved at every step. Each node can be tested on its own with a hand-written state dictionary. This shape (deterministic skeleton, model at the decision points) is what most production \"agents\" look like inside.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Where you will meet graphs\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>LangGraph\u003c/b> (Python and JavaScript): a widely used, low-level graph runtime from LangChain. LangChain's \u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code> and the Deep Agents harness are built on it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Google ADK 2.0\u003c/b> graph workflows and \u003cb>Microsoft Agent Framework\u003c/b> workflows: the same ideas inside vendor frameworks (lesson 9).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pydantic Graph\u003c/b>, \u003cb>LlamaIndex Workflows\u003c/b> (event-driven steps) and \u003cb>Mastra\u003c/b> workflows (TypeScript).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Durable execution engines\u003c/b> (Temporal, Restate, DBOS, Inngest) express workflows as ordinary code rather than as graphs, but solve the same reliability problem (lesson 8).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-note\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Note\u003c/p>\u003cp>\"Graph\" also means knowledge graphs (GraphRAG, covered in Advanced retrieval) and graph-shaped memory (Zep and Graphiti, covered in Memory). Those are graphs of \u003ci>facts\u003c/i>. This module is about graphs of \u003ci>control flow\u003c/i>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is graph engineering, in one sentence?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Designing an agent workflow as an explicit state machine of nodes, edges and shared state, so that deterministic code enforces the steps that must always happen and the model makes only the decisions that need judgement.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name four signals that a free agent loop is the wrong design for a use case.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any four of: mandatory steps that must never be skipped; actions that need human approval; runs that must survive restarts or wait for days; work that can run in parallel; steps that need different models, prompts or tools; a need for step-by-step audit; a need for predictable cost and latency.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do agent graphs allow cycles when Airflow-style pipelines do not?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Agent workflows naturally contain loops: the model-tools loop, retry until a validator passes, revise until a reviewer approves. A DAG cannot express \"go back to the draft step\", so agent graph runtimes support cycles and bound them with counters and recursion limits instead.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:langgraph-graphs-state-and-persistence":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A LangGraph program has three parts: a \u003cb>state schema\u003c/b> (usually a \u003ccode>TypedDict\u003c/code>), \u003cb>nodes\u003c/b> (functions that take the state and return a partial update) and \u003cb>edges\u003c/b> (which node runs next). You add them to a \u003ccode>StateGraph\u003c/code>, call \u003ccode>compile()\u003c/code>, and run it with \u003ccode>invoke()\u003c/code>. Passing a \u003cb>checkpointer\u003c/b> to \u003ccode>compile()\u003c/code> and a \u003ccode>thread_id\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>invoke()\u003c/code> saves the state after every step, which is what makes a run resumable.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>A first graph\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The example below is a three-step pipeline for a support ticket: extract the order number, look the order up, draft a reply. It runs without any API key; in a real system \u003ccode>draft\u003c/code> would call a model.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import re\nfrom typing import TypedDict\nfrom langgraph.graph import StateGraph, START, END\n\nORDERS = {\"42\": \"shipped on 3 March\", \"77\": \"awaiting stock\"}\n\nclass State(TypedDict, total=False):\n    message: str      # input\n    order_id: str     # written by extract\n    status: str       # written by lookup\n    reply: str        # written by draft\n\ndef extract(state: State) -&gt; dict:\n    m = re.search(r\"\\b(\\d+)\\b\", state[\"message\"])\n    return {\"order_id\": m.group(1) if m else \"\"}\n\ndef lookup(state: State) -&gt; dict:\n    return {\"status\": ORDERS.get(state[\"order_id\"], \"not found\")}\n\ndef draft(state: State) -&gt; dict:\n    return {\"reply\": f\"Order {state['order_id']} is {state['status']}.\"}\n\nbuilder = StateGraph(State)\nbuilder.add_node(\"extract\", extract)\nbuilder.add_node(\"lookup\", lookup)\nbuilder.add_node(\"draft\", draft)\nbuilder.add_edge(START, \"extract\")\nbuilder.add_edge(\"extract\", \"lookup\")\nbuilder.add_edge(\"lookup\", \"draft\")\nbuilder.add_edge(\"draft\", END)\ngraph = builder.compile()\n\nprint(graph.invoke({\"message\": \"Where is order 42?\"}))\n# {'message': 'Where is order 42?', 'order_id': '42', 'status': 'shipped on 3 March',\n#  'reply': 'Order 42 is shipped on 3 March.'}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What happens when you call invoke\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>The input dictionary is written into the state.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>START\u003c/code> is a virtual node; its edge triggers \u003ccode>extract\u003c/code> in the first super-step.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>extract\u003c/code> receives the whole state and returns only the key it changed, \u003ccode>{\"order_id\": \"42\"}\u003c/code>. This is a \u003cb>partial update\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>LangGraph applies the update with each key's \u003cb>reducer\u003c/b>. With no reducer declared, the default is \u003ci>overwrite\u003c/i>: the new value replaces the old one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The edge from \u003ccode>extract\u003c/code> triggers \u003ccode>lookup\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>draft\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>END\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>invoke\u003c/code> returns the final state.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Nodes never mutate the state object they receive; they return updates and the runtime merges them. That rule is what makes checkpointing, parallel branches and replay possible.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Nodes can be anything\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A node is any function (sync or async) that takes the state and returns a dictionary of updates. Inside it you can call a model, run a SQL query, call a whole agent built with \u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code>, or invoke another compiled graph. Nodes can also receive a \u003ccode>config\u003c/code> (which carries the \u003ccode>thread_id\u003c/code>) and a \u003ccode>runtime\u003c/code> whose \u003ccode>context\u003c/code> holds per-run dependencies such as a database client or the model name. Pass those through \u003ccode>context_schema\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>invoke(..., context=...)\u003c/code> rather than putting them in state, because state is saved to the checkpointer and must be serialisable.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Compiling, threads and checkpoints\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>compile()\u003c/code> checks the structure (for example, that no node is unreachable) and attaches runtime options. The most important option is the checkpointer:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import sqlite3\nfrom langgraph.checkpoint.sqlite import SqliteSaver   # pip install langgraph-checkpoint-sqlite\n\nconn = sqlite3.connect(\"graph.db\", check_same_thread=False)\ngraph = builder.compile(checkpointer=SqliteSaver(conn))\n\ncfg = {\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": \"ticket-1234\"}}\ngraph.invoke({\"message\": \"Where is order 42?\"}, cfg)\nprint(graph.get_state(cfg).values[\"reply\"])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A \u003cb>thread\u003c/b> is one run history, identified by \u003ccode>thread_id\u003c/code>. The checkpointer writes a snapshot at every super-step boundary: for this graph, five in total (two while the input is received and written into the state, then one after each of the three nodes). If the process dies after \u003ccode>lookup\u003c/code>, calling \u003ccode>graph.invoke(None, cfg)\u003c/code> later continues with \u003ccode>draft\u003c/code> instead of starting again. Lesson 5 covers checkpoints in depth.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Typed state.\u003c/b> Use \u003ccode>TypedDict\u003c/code> (fast, the documented default), a dataclass when you want defaults, or a Pydantic model when you want validation at every update (slower).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Small, testable nodes.\u003c/b> Because a node is a function from state to update, you can unit-test it with a hand-written dictionary and no model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Look at the graph.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>graph.get_graph().draw_mermaid()\u003c/code> prints a Mermaid diagram; LangSmith Studio shows the same graph live with each step's state.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Input and output schemas.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>StateGraph(State, input_schema=..., output_schema=...)\u003c/code> keeps internal keys out of the public interface.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pin versions.\u003c/b> LangGraph reached 1.0 in late 2025 and keeps a stable core API, but helper APIs (streaming, middleware) still evolve; pin the version and read the changelog when upgrading.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Model a workflow as a graph with checkpoints: what do you define, and what makes it checkpointed?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Define a state schema (a \u003ccode>TypedDict\u003c/code> listing every field the workflow reads or writes), one node function per step that returns a partial update, and edges from \u003ccode>START\u003c/code> through the steps to \u003ccode>END\u003c/code> (conditional edges where the path depends on the state). Compile it with a checkpointer, for example \u003ccode>builder.compile(checkpointer=SqliteSaver(conn))\u003c/code> locally or \u003ccode>PostgresSaver\u003c/code> in production, and invoke it with \u003ccode>{\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": \"...\"}}\u003c/code>. The runtime then saves a snapshot after every super-step; \u003ccode>get_state(cfg)\u003c/code> shows the latest one and \u003ccode>invoke(None, cfg)\u003c/code> resumes an interrupted or crashed run from the last saved step.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A node returns \u003ccode>{\"status\": \"shipped\"}\u003c/code> but the state also has \u003ccode>message\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>order_id\u003c/code>. What happens to those keys?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Nothing. Node returns are partial updates; only \u003ccode>status\u003c/code> is passed to its reducer (overwrite by default), and every other key keeps its current value.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should a database client be passed as runtime context rather than stored in state?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>State is serialised into the checkpointer after every step, so it must contain plain data. A client object cannot be serialised and would also leak connection details into stored checkpoints; runtime context is supplied fresh on each invocation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/conditional-edges-routing-and-cycles":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>conditional edge\u003c/b> runs a routing function after a node and goes wherever it says: another node, several nodes in parallel, or \u003ccode>END\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>Command\u003c/code> lets a node update the state and choose the next node in one return value. A \u003cb>cycle\u003c/b> is an edge back to an earlier node; every cycle needs an exit condition in the state plus the runtime's recursion limit as a safety net.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Conditional edges\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from typing import Literal\n\ndef route(state: State) -&gt; Literal[\"answer\", \"refund\"]:\n    return \"refund\" if state[\"intent\"] == \"refund\" else \"answer\"\n\nbuilder.add_edge(START, \"classify\")\nbuilder.add_conditional_edges(\"classify\", route)   # after classify, call route()\nbuilder.add_edge(\"answer\", END)\nbuilder.add_edge(\"refund\", END)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The routing function receives the state and returns the name of the next node, a list of names (all run in parallel in the next super-step), or \u003ccode>END\u003c/code>. The \u003ccode>Literal[...]\u003c/code> return annotation is optional but lets LangGraph draw the possible branches. You can instead pass a mapping such as \u003ccode>{\"yes\": \"refund\", \"no\": \"answer\"}\u003c/code> as a third argument when the router returns labels rather than node names.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Routers should be cheap and deterministic where possible. A common layering:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rules first\u003c/b>: an order number plus the word \"refund\" goes straight to the refund branch.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A small model next\u003c/b>: a classifier prompt with structured output (\u003ccode>{\"route\": \"refund\" | \"question\" | \"other\"}\u003c/code>) for everything rules cannot decide.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A fallback route\u003c/b>: low confidence or \"other\" goes to a clarifying question or a human, never to a random branch.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Evaluate an LLM router like any classifier: label 50 to 100 real messages and read the confusion matrix. Misroutes are the most common silent failure in graph systems.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Command: update and route in one step\u003c/h4>\u003cp>When the node that makes a decision also produces data, return a \u003ccode>Command\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from langgraph.types import Command\n\ndef classify(state: State) -&gt; Command[Literal[\"answer\", \"refund\"]]:\n    intent = \"refund\" if \"refund\" in state[\"message\"].lower() else \"question\"\n    return Command(update={\"intent\": intent},\n                   goto=\"refund\" if intent == \"refund\" else \"answer\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The \u003ccode>Command[Literal[...]]\u003c/code> annotation is required so the graph knows the possible destinations. Do not also add a normal edge from the same node: static edges still run, so both the static target and the \u003ccode>goto\u003c/code> target would execute.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cycles that terminate\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A revise loop: write a draft, check it, and go back to writing until it passes or a budget runs out.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class Draft(TypedDict):\n    text: str\n    attempts: int\n    passed: bool\n\ndef write(s: Draft) -&gt; dict:\n    return {\"text\": improve(s[\"text\"]), \"attempts\": s[\"attempts\"] + 1}   # LLM call\n\ndef check(s: Draft) -&gt; dict:\n    return {\"passed\": validator(s[\"text\"])}            # tests, schema, or a judge\n\ndef after_check(s: Draft) -&gt; Literal[\"write\", \"__end__\"]:\n    if s[\"passed\"] or s[\"attempts\"] &gt;= 3:\n        return END\n    return \"write\"\n\nb = StateGraph(Draft)\nb.add_node(\"write\", write)\nb.add_node(\"check\", check)\nb.add_edge(START, \"write\")\nb.add_edge(\"write\", \"check\")\nb.add_conditional_edges(\"check\", after_check)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two layers of protection:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Your own exit condition\u003c/b>, stored in state (\u003ccode>attempts &gt;= 3\u003c/code>), so the graph ends gracefully with its best draft and a clear status.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The recursion limit\u003c/b>, a cap on super-steps per run. When it is exceeded LangGraph raises \u003ccode>GraphRecursionError\u003c/code>. Since LangGraph 1.0.6 the default is 1,000 super-steps; set a tighter one per call with \u003ccode>graph.invoke(inputs, {\"recursion_limit\": 25})\u003c/code>. A \u003ccode>RemainingSteps\u003c/code> field in the state lets a node see how many steps are left and wrap up before the limit.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>The agent loop as a graph\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The model-tools loop is itself a two-node cycle. LangGraph ships prebuilt pieces for it: \u003ccode>ToolNode\u003c/code> runs the tool calls found in the last message, and \u003ccode>tools_condition\u003c/code> routes to \u003ccode>\"tools\"\u003c/code> when the model asked for a tool and to \u003ccode>END\u003c/code> otherwise:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from langgraph.prebuilt import ToolNode, tools_condition\n\nb.add_node(\"model\", call_model)              # calls a chat model bound to your tools\nb.add_node(\"tools\", ToolNode([search_docs, get_order]))\nb.add_edge(START, \"model\")\nb.add_conditional_edges(\"model\", tools_condition)\nb.add_edge(\"tools\", \"model\")                  # the cycle\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Most teams use \u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code> from LangChain for this loop (Frameworks module) and drop the resulting agent into a bigger graph as a single node.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A router that returns a name that is not a node fails at run time; enumerate routes with \u003ccode>Literal\u003c/code> and test every branch.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A cycle with no exit condition in state relies on the recursion limit, which ends the run with an exception instead of a useful answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Retry loops that re-send the whole growing history get more expensive on every lap; pass the checker's feedback, not the full transcript.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What may a routing function return, and what happens in each case?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A node name (that node runs next), a list of node names (they all run in parallel in the next super-step), or \u003ccode>END\u003c/code> (this branch finishes). With a path mapping, it may return a label that the mapping translates into a node name.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When should a node return \u003ccode>Command\u003c/code> instead of relying on a conditional edge?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the same function both updates state and decides where to go, for example a classifier that stores the intent and routes on it. If you only need routing without updates, a conditional edge is simpler. Never combine \u003ccode>Command\u003c/code> routing with a static edge from the same node.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your revise loop sometimes runs 40 laps and costs dollars per request. Name two fixes.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Add an attempts counter to the state and exit after a small number of laps (returning the best draft and a status); set a tight \u003ccode>recursion_limit\u003c/code> per invocation as a backstop. Also pass only the checker's feedback, not the full history, into each rewrite to stop per-lap cost growing.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/state-design-and-reducers":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every state key has a \u003cb>reducer\u003c/b>: the function that combines the current value with a node's update. The default overwrites; \u003ccode>operator.add\u003c/code> appends lists; \u003ccode>add_messages\u003c/code> appends chat messages and replaces ones with the same id. Reducers are what make \u003cb>parallel branches\u003c/b> safe: two nodes running in the same super-step may write to one key only if a reducer says how to merge their values.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How updates are applied\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each key a node returns, LangGraph computes \u003ccode>new_value = reducer(current_value, update)\u003c/code>. You attach a reducer with \u003ccode>Annotated\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import operator\nfrom typing import Annotated, TypedDict\nfrom langgraph.graph.message import add_messages\n\nclass State(TypedDict):\n    question: str                                   # default: overwrite\n    hits: Annotated[list[str], operator.add]        # append lists\n    messages: Annotated[list, add_messages]         # chat history\n    cost_usd: Annotated[float, operator.add]        # running total\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Key\u003c/th>\u003cth>Current value\u003c/th>\u003cth>Node returns\u003c/th>\u003cth>New value\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>question\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"vpn setup\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"vpn on mac\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"vpn on mac\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>hits\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>[\"doc-1\"]\u003c/td>\u003ctd>[\"doc-7\"]\u003c/td>\u003ctd>[\"doc-1\", \"doc-7\"]\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>cost_usd\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.002\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.003\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.005\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two details catch people out. First, returning an empty list to an appending key does not clear it, because \u003ccode>[] + old\u003c/code> is still \u003ccode>old\u003c/code>; wrap the update as \u003ccode>Overwrite([])\u003c/code> (from \u003ccode>langgraph.types\u003c/code>) to bypass the reducer. Second, a custom reducer is just a function of \u003ccode>(left, right)\u003c/code>, so you can write one that keeps the ten best hits by score or merges two dictionaries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Messages\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Chat history is the most common key, and plain list appending is not quite enough for it. \u003ccode>add_messages\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>appends new messages;\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>replaces\u003c/b> an existing message when the update carries the same message id, which is how a human edits a proposed tool call before it runs;\u003c/li>\u003cli>accepts plain dictionaries such as \u003ccode>{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"hi\"}\u003c/code> and converts them to LangChain message objects (so read them as \u003ccode>msg.content\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>msg[\"content\"]\u003c/code>);\u003c/li>\u003cli>deletes a message when you return \u003ccode>RemoveMessage(id=...)\u003c/code>, which is how you trim history.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>\u003ccode>MessagesState\u003c/code> is a prebuilt state with one \u003ccode>messages\u003c/code> key using this reducer; subclass it to add your own fields. History strategies (windows, summaries, compaction) are covered in the Memory module.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Parallel branches: fan-out and fan-in\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A node with several outgoing edges triggers all of them in the next super-step. An edge from a \u003ci>list\u003c/i> of nodes waits for all of them before running its target:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def search_docs(s):    return {\"hits\": [f\"doc result for {s['query']}\"]}\ndef search_tickets(s): return {\"hits\": [f\"ticket result for {s['query']}\"]}\ndef merge(s):          return {\"answer\": f\"{len(s['hits'])} hits: \" + \"; \".join(s[\"hits\"])}\n\nb = StateGraph(State)\nb.add_node(\"search_docs\", search_docs)\nb.add_node(\"search_tickets\", search_tickets)\nb.add_node(\"merge\", merge)\nb.add_edge(START, \"search_docs\")                       # fan-out\nb.add_edge(START, \"search_tickets\")\nb.add_edge([\"search_docs\", \"search_tickets\"], \"merge\")   # fan-in: waits for both\nb.add_edge(\"merge\", END)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Both searches run in the same super-step and both write \u003ccode>hits\u003c/code>. With the \u003ccode>operator.add\u003c/code> reducer the result is two hits. Without it, the run fails with \u003ccode>InvalidUpdateError: At key 'hits': Can receive only one value per step. Use an Annotated key to handle multiple values.\u003c/code> That error is LangGraph protecting you from silently losing one branch's output.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Dynamic fan-out with Send\u003c/h4>\u003cp>When the number of branches is only known at run time (one per document, one per sub-question), return a list of \u003ccode>Send\u003c/code> objects from a conditional edge. Each \u003ccode>Send\u003c/code> names a node and gives it its own small input:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from langgraph.types import Send\n\nclass One(TypedDict):\n    q: str\n\ndef fan_out(state) -&gt; list[Send]:\n    return [Send(\"answer_one\", {\"q\": q}) for q in state[\"questions\"][:10]]   # cap the fan-out\n\ndef answer_one(s: One) -&gt; dict:\n    return {\"answers\": [call_model(s[\"q\"])]}         # merged by the answers reducer\n\nb.add_conditional_edges(\"plan\", fan_out, [\"answer_one\"])\nb.add_edge(\"answer_one\", \"combine\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>This is the map-reduce pattern behind most \"research\" graphs: plan, fan out N searches or summaries, combine. Cap N, because cost grows linearly with it, and remember that parallel calls also multiply your rate-limit usage.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Designing the state\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Good state design prevents most graph bugs:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Store what later steps need\u003c/b>, not everything you computed along the way.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keep it small and serialisable.\u003c/b> Every checkpoint stores the state, so put large documents in a store or object storage and keep an id in state.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Separate conversation from facts.\u003c/b> Keep messages in \u003ccode>messages\u003c/code> and decisions in typed keys (\u003ccode>order_id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>amount\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>approved\u003c/code>). Routing on a typed field is far more reliable than asking a model to re-read the transcript.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One writer per key\u003c/b> where possible. Several writers need a reducer and a reason.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Do not rely on the order of parallel results.\u003c/b> Key them by source or sort them before use.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hide internals.\u003c/b> Private keys and input and output schemas keep the public interface small.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Key\u003c/th>\u003cth>Type\u003c/th>\u003cth>Reducer\u003c/th>\u003cth>Written by\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>messages\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>list of messages\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>add_messages\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>user input, \u003ccode>answer\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>reply\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>intent\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"question\", \"refund\" or \"other\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>overwrite\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>classify\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>order_id\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>string\u003c/td>\u003ctd>overwrite\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>extract\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>hits\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>list of document ids\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>operator.add\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>parallel searches\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>approved\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>bool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>overwrite\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>approval\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Two parallel nodes both return \u003ccode>{\"hits\": [...]}\u003c/code> and the run fails with \u003ccode>InvalidUpdateError\u003c/code>. Why, and how do you fix it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Both nodes wrote the same key in the same super-step and the key has the default overwrite reducer, so LangGraph cannot know which value to keep and refuses rather than dropping one. Annotate the key with a merging reducer, for example \u003ccode>Annotated[list[str], operator.add]\u003c/code>, or give each branch its own key.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A node returns a message whose id matches an existing message in \u003ccode>messages\u003c/code>. What does \u003ccode>add_messages\u003c/code> do, and why is that useful?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It replaces the existing message instead of appending a duplicate. That lets a reviewer or a node edit a previous message, for example correcting the arguments of a proposed tool call before it executes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why store a document id in state rather than the 40-page document text?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>State is copied into every checkpoint and passed to every node, so large values bloat storage, slow each step and leak into traces. A reference keeps checkpoints small and lets nodes fetch only what they need.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/checkpoints-persistence-and-time-travel":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>checkpointer\u003c/b> saves a snapshot of the state at every super-step, grouped into \u003cb>threads\u003c/b> by \u003ccode>thread_id\u003c/code>. That one mechanism gives you conversation memory (reuse the thread id), crash recovery (resume from the last snapshot), human-in-the-loop (pause now, resume tomorrow) and \u003cb>time travel\u003c/b> (inspect, replay or fork any earlier step).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What a snapshot contains\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>graph.get_state(cfg)\u003c/code> returns the latest \u003ccode>StateSnapshot\u003c/code> of a thread; \u003ccode>graph.get_state_history(cfg)\u003c/code> returns all of them, newest first.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Field\u003c/th>\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>values\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The state at this point\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>next\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nodes that will run next; an empty tuple means the run finished\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>config\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>thread_id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>checkpoint_ns\u003c/code> and this snapshot's \u003ccode>checkpoint_id\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>metadata\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>source\u003c/code> (\"input\", \"loop\" or \"update\"), \u003ccode>step\u003c/code> number and the writes made\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tasks\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pending tasks, with any error or interrupt they hit\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>parent_config\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The previous snapshot, so history forms a chain\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Here is the history of a refund run that paused for approval and was then approved:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>step  source  next\n  3   loop    ()                 finished\n  2   loop    ('execute',)\n  1   loop    ('approve',)       the run waited here for a human\n  0   loop    ('plan',)\n -1   input   ('__start__',)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Choosing a checkpointer\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Checkpointer\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use for\u003c/th>\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>InMemorySaver\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tests and notebooks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Lost when the process exits\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>SqliteSaver\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>AsyncSqliteSaver\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Local development, single-process tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Package \u003ccode>langgraph-checkpoint-sqlite\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PostgresSaver\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>AsyncPostgresSaver\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Production\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Package \u003ccode>langgraph-checkpoint-postgres\u003c/code>; call \u003ccode>setup()\u003c/code> once to create tables\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>MongoDB, Azure Cosmos DB and others\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Production on those stores\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Community and vendor packages\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LangSmith Agent Server\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Managed deployment\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Persistence is configured for you\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Crash recovery\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Checkpoints are written at super-step boundaries, so a failure loses at most the step in progress:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>cfg = {\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": \"job-1\"}}\ntry:\n    graph.invoke(inputs, cfg)          # node \"nb\" raises: provider timeout\nexcept RuntimeError:\n    print(graph.get_state(cfg).next)   # ('nb',)\ngraph.invoke(None, cfg)                # resumes at nb; earlier nodes are not re-run\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Passing \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> as input means \"continue this thread from where it stopped\". If several nodes ran in parallel and only one failed, the successful siblings' outputs are stored as \u003cb>pending writes\u003c/b>, so on resume only the failed node runs again. For transient errors, add a retry policy to the node, \u003ccode>builder.add_node(\"call_api\", call_api, retry_policy=RetryPolicy(max_attempts=3))\u003c/code>, which retries with exponential backoff before giving up.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-warn\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Watch out\u003c/p>\u003cp>A checkpointer saves state; it does not restart anything. If your server dies, something must later call \u003ccode>invoke(None, cfg)\u003c/code> for every unfinished thread: a worker queue, a sweep job that finds threads whose latest snapshot has non-empty \u003ccode>next\u003c/code>, or a managed runtime such as the LangSmith Agent Server. Lesson 8 compares this with durable execution engines that handle recovery for you.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Durability modes\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You can trade write overhead for safety per call, for example \u003ccode>graph.invoke(inputs, cfg, durability=\"sync\")\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\u003cth>When state is written\u003c/th>\u003cth>Risk\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>\"exit\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only when the run finishes, errors or interrupts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A crash mid-run loses all intermediate steps\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>\"async\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>In the background while the next step runs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A crash can lose the latest write\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>\"sync\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Before the next step starts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Safest; small latency cost per step\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Time travel: replay and fork\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>history = list(graph.get_state_history(cfg))                # newest first\nbefore = next(s for s in history if s.next == (\"approve\",))\n\ngraph.invoke(None, before.config)                            # replay from that point\n\nfork_cfg = graph.update_state(before.config, {\"plan\": \"refund half of order 42\"})\ngraph.invoke(None, fork_cfg)                                 # fork with edited state\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Replay\u003c/b> resumes from an old checkpoint. Nodes before it are not re-run; nodes after it \u003ci>are\u003c/i> re-executed, so model calls fire again and may give different answers, and interrupts pause again.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fork\u003c/b> uses \u003ccode>update_state\u003c/code> to write a new checkpoint that branches from the old one, as if a node had produced the edit (\u003ccode>as_node=\u003c/code> chooses which node, which decides what runs next). The original history stays intact, and the fork becomes the thread's latest state.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Teams use time travel to debug (\"what would have happened if the classifier had said refund?\"), to repair a run after a bad tool result by editing the state, and to turn real production states into eval cases.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Thread ids\u003c/b>: one per conversation, ticket or job; readable ids such as \u003ccode>ticket-1234\u003c/code> help support staff. Keep them under 255 characters for Postgres.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Retention\u003c/b>: checkpoints accumulate on every step. Prune finished threads on a schedule.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sensitive data\u003c/b>: checkpoints contain everything in state, including personal data and tool results. Encrypt them (LangGraph provides an \u003ccode>EncryptedSerializer\u003c/code>), set retention, and keep secrets in runtime context, never in state.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Changing the graph\u003c/b>: adding nodes and keys is safe for existing threads; renaming or removing a node can break threads currently paused in it, and renamed keys lose their saved values.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Checkpointer versus store\u003c/b>: a checkpointer is short-term, per-thread memory; a \u003cb>store\u003c/b> holds long-term memory shared across threads (Memory module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your server restarted while 30 graph runs were mid-flight. What do you have with a Postgres checkpointer, and what must you still build?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each thread's state up to its last completed super-step is safe in Postgres, but nothing resumes by itself. You need a recovery job (or a managed runtime) that finds threads whose latest snapshot still has nodes in \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> and no pending interrupt, then calls \u003ccode>invoke(None, cfg)\u003c/code> for each. The node that was running at the crash runs again from its start, so it must be idempotent.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between replay and fork?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Replay continues from an old checkpoint with the state exactly as it was, re-executing the later nodes. Fork first writes an edited state with \u003ccode>update_state\u003c/code>, creating a new branch from that checkpoint, then continues from the edit. Neither deletes the original history.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is \u003ccode>InMemorySaver\u003c/code> unsafe in production even if your graph works perfectly in tests?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It keeps checkpoints in process memory, so a restart, deploy or crash loses every thread's state, including conversations and runs waiting for approval, and it cannot be shared across several server processes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/interrupts-human-approval-inside-a-graph":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Calling \u003ccode>interrupt(payload)\u003c/code> inside a node pauses the graph: the state is checkpointed, the payload is returned to your application, and the thread waits, indefinitely, until you invoke it again with \u003ccode>Command(resume=value)\u003c/code>. The paused node then runs again \u003cb>from its first line\u003c/b>, and this time \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>value\u003c/code>. That restart-from-the-top behaviour explains every rule for writing approval nodes safely.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pause and resume\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from typing import Literal\nfrom langgraph.types import interrupt, Command\n\ndef approve(state: State) -&gt; Command[Literal[\"execute\", \"reject\"]]:\n    decision = interrupt({\"action\": state[\"plan\"], \"question\": \"Approve this refund?\"})\n    return Command(update={\"approved\": decision[\"approved\"]},\n                   goto=\"execute\" if decision[\"approved\"] else \"reject\")\n\ngraph = builder.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)      # a checkpointer is required\ncfg = {\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": \"ticket-123\"}}\n\nout = graph.invoke({\"request\": \"order 42 arrived broken\"}, cfg)\nprint(out[\"__interrupt__\"][0].value)\n# {'action': 'refund order in: order 42 arrived broken', 'question': 'Approve this refund?'}\n\n# hours later, possibly in another process with the same checkpointer:\nout = graph.invoke(Command(resume={\"approved\": True}), cfg)\nprint(out[\"result\"])     # refund issued\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Three things are required: a checkpointer, a \u003ccode>thread_id\u003c/code>, and a JSON-serialisable payload. The newer streaming interface, \u003ccode>graph.stream_events(..., version=\"v3\")\u003c/code>, exposes the same information as \u003ccode>stream.interrupted\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>stream.interrupts\u003c/code>; plain \u003ccode>invoke\u003c/code> returns it under the \u003ccode>__interrupt__\u003c/code> key.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The node runs again from the top\u003c/h4>\u003cp>If you add a \u003ccode>print\u003c/code> at the start of \u003ccode>approve\u003c/code>, it prints twice: once before pausing and once on resume. LangGraph pauses by raising a special exception inside \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code>, saves the checkpoint, and on resume re-executes the node, feeding the stored resume value to the matching \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> call. Hence the rules:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Rule\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Put side effects after \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> or in a later node\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Code before it runs again on every resume\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Make anything before it idempotent (upserts, idempotency keys)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Otherwise records are duplicated\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Never wrap \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> in a bare \u003ccode>try/except Exception\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You would catch the pause signal and the graph would not stop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Keep the sequence of \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> calls in a node identical on every execution\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Resume values are matched to calls by position, so skipping one based on changing data misaligns them\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pass simple JSON payloads, not objects or functions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The payload is stored in the checkpoint\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Approval patterns\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Approve or reject\u003c/b>: route with \u003ccode>Command(goto=...)\u003c/code> on the decision, as above.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Edit\u003c/b>: resume with corrected values, for example \u003ccode>{\"approved\": True, \"amount\": 40}\u003c/code>, and validate the edit in code, because reviewers make typos too.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Review tool calls\u003c/b>: place an approval node between the model and tools nodes. With LangChain's prebuilt agent, \u003ccode>HumanInTheLoopMiddleware(interrupt_on={\"issue_refund\": {\"allowed_decisions\": [\"approve\", \"edit\", \"reject\"]}})\u003c/code> does this for you.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Several approvals at once\u003c/b>: when parallel branches each interrupt, resume all of them with one dictionary mapping each interrupt's \u003ccode>id\u003c/code> to its value.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Typed answers\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>interrupt(payload, response_schema=Decision)\u003c/code> (LangGraph 1.2.12 or later) validates the resume value against a Pydantic model and gives your UI a schema to render as a form.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>For debugging only, \u003ccode>compile(interrupt_before=[\"node\"])\u003c/code> sets static breakpoints that pause before a node; resume with \u003ccode>invoke(None, cfg)\u003c/code>. The docs recommend \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> for real human-in-the-loop flows because it can be conditional and carries a payload.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Designing approval as a product feature\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The mechanism is a few lines; the hard part is the human side.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Show exactly what will happen\u003c/b>, in words and parameters: \"Refund 80.00 EUR to the card ending 4417 for order 42\", plus the evidence the agent used.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Decide who may approve what\u003c/b>, in code: for example automatic under 20 EUR, any support agent up to 200, a manager above.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Waiting is free for the graph but not for the customer.\u003c/b> A paused thread waits forever, so add reminders and an expiry job that resumes stale approvals with a rejection after, say, 24 hours.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Record the decision, reviewer and payload\u003c/b> in an audit log outside the graph.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Measure approval and edit rates.\u003c/b> A high edit rate shows exactly where the agent needs work. When to require approval at all is covered in the Agents module.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">An approval node writes an audit record \"approval requested\" and then calls \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code>. Each approval produces two audit records. Why, and how do you fix it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>On resume the node re-runs from its first line, so the audit write before \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> executes again. Move the write after \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> (recording the decision), put it in a separate node before the approval node, or make it idempotent with an upsert keyed on the thread and step.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What three things must be in place for \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> to work?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The graph must be compiled with a checkpointer, every invocation must carry the same \u003ccode>thread_id\u003c/code> in its config, and the payload passed to \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> must be JSON-serialisable.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why are static \u003ccode>interrupt_before\u003c/code> breakpoints not recommended for approvals?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>They always pause before a node regardless of the state and carry no payload, so you cannot pause only for risky cases or show the reviewer what to approve. \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> can be called conditionally inside the node with exactly the information the reviewer needs.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/subgraphs-and-multi-agent-graphs-supervisor-and-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>subgraph\u003c/b> is a compiled graph used as a node in another graph. Subgraphs let teams build and test parts separately and are how multi-agent systems are wired. In a \u003cb>supervisor\u003c/b> design one coordinating model decides which specialist runs next and every specialist reports back to it. In a \u003cb>swarm\u003c/b> (handoff) design specialists pass control directly to each other and the last active agent keeps talking to the user.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Two ways to plug in a subgraph\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Pattern\u003c/th>\u003cth>When\u003c/th>\u003cth>How\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Add the compiled subgraph as a node\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Parent and child share state keys\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>parent.add_node(\"research\", research_graph)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Call the subgraph inside a node function\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Different schemas, or you want to transform input and output\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>out = child.invoke({\"text\": s[\"question\"]})\u003c/code> then return a parent update\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class Research(TypedDict):\n    question: str\n    notes: list\n\nsub = StateGraph(Research)\nsub.add_node(\"search\", lambda s: {\"notes\": [f\"note about {s['question']}\"]})\nsub.add_edge(START, \"search\")\nresearch = sub.compile()                         # no checkpointer needed here\n\nparent = StateGraph(Parent)                      # Parent also has question and notes\nparent.add_node(\"research\", research)            # shared keys flow in and out\nparent.add_node(\"write\", write)\nparent.add_edge(START, \"research\")\nparent.add_edge(\"research\", \"write\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Give the checkpointer only to the parent; subgraphs inherit it and store their checkpoints under their own \u003cb>checkpoint namespace\u003c/b>, so you can inspect a child's state separately. An \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> inside a subgraph pauses the whole parent run, and on resume both the parent node that called the subgraph and the interrupted child node re-run from their start.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The call-inside-a-node pattern is the usual choice for agents, because it lets each agent keep a \u003cb>private message history\u003c/b> and return only a summary to the parent. That is context isolation, the main reason multi-agent designs exist (Multi-agent module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The supervisor pattern\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>User\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Supervisor\u003c/b>\u003csmall>picks next worker or finish\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Worker\u003c/b>\u003csmall>researcher, writer or coder\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Supervisor\u003c/b>\u003csmall>reads result, decides again\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Answer\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>As a graph, the supervisor is a node that returns \u003ccode>Command(goto=worker)\u003c/code>, and every worker has a normal edge back to the supervisor:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def supervisor(s: Team) -&gt; Command[Literal[\"researcher\", \"writer\", \"__end__\"]]:\n    choice = pick_next(s)              # LLM with structured output: researcher, writer or FINISH\n    if choice == \"FINISH\" or s[\"turns\"] &gt;= 6:\n        return Command(goto=END)\n    return Command(goto=choice, update={\"turns\": s[\"turns\"] + 1})\n\nb.add_node(\"supervisor\", supervisor)\nb.add_node(\"researcher\", researcher)   # each may itself be an agent or a subgraph\nb.add_node(\"writer\", writer)\nb.add_edge(START, \"supervisor\")\nb.add_edge(\"researcher\", \"supervisor\")\nb.add_edge(\"writer\", \"supervisor\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A simpler variant, now recommended in the LangChain docs over the older \u003ccode>langgraph-supervisor\u003c/code> library, is \u003cb>subagents as tools\u003c/b>: the coordinator is an ordinary tool-calling agent whose tools each invoke a specialist agent and return its summary. It gives you more control over exactly what each specialist sees.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The swarm (handoff) pattern\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In a swarm there is no coordinator. Each agent has \u003cb>handoff tools\u003c/b> such as \u003ccode>transfer_to_tech\u003c/code>; calling one hands control to that agent, and an \u003ccode>active_agent\u003c/code> key in the state remembers who is talking so the next user message goes straight to them. Inside a subgraph, a handoff is a \u003ccode>Command\u003c/code> aimed at the parent graph:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def billing_step(s: Chat) -&gt; Command:\n    return Command(goto=\"tech\", graph=Command.PARENT,\n                   update={\"log\": [\"billing: looks like a bug, handing to tech\"], \"active\": \"tech\"})\n\nparent.add_node(\"billing\", billing_agent, destinations=(\"tech\",))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two details matter. When a subgraph updates a parent key that both share, the parent key should have a reducer. When the handoff is a tool call, the update must include a \u003ccode>ToolMessage\u003c/code> answering that call, or the message history becomes invalid for the next model call. The \u003ccode>langgraph-swarm\u003c/code> package wraps this pattern.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What each pattern costs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>LangChain's multi-agent guide counts model calls for a simple request (\"buy coffee\") handled by a specialist:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Pattern\u003c/th>\u003cth>Calls, first request\u003c/th>\u003cth>Calls, same request repeated\u003c/th>\u003cth>Strength\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Subagents (supervisor via tools)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4 more (8 total)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Central control, strong context isolation, parallel work\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Handoffs (swarm)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2 more (5 total)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The specialist keeps the conversation; fewer calls\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Router (classify, then one agent)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3 more (6 total)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Simple, parallel fan-out to several agents\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For a multi-domain question (\"compare Python, JavaScript and Rust for web work\"), the same guide reports about 5 calls and 9K tokens for subagents against 7 or more calls and 14K or more tokens for handoffs, because the coordinator can fan out in parallel while handoffs go one agent at a time. No pattern wins everywhere: measure on your traffic.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Start with one agent and good tools. Split into agents only when a single agent has too many tools to choose well, when parts need very different context, or when work can run in parallel.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Decide what each agent returns: a concise result with sources, not its whole transcript.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Put budgets on the coordinator (maximum turns, maximum workers) as well as on each worker.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Trace across graphs: LangSmith and OpenTelemetry tracing show subgraph runs nested under the parent run, which is essential for finding which agent went wrong.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you call a subgraph inside a node function instead of adding it directly as a node?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the subgraph has a different state schema from the parent, or when you want to transform what goes in and out, typically to give an agent its own private message history and return only a summary to the parent.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Contrast the supervisor and swarm patterns in two sentences.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In a supervisor design every turn passes through a coordinator that chooses the next specialist and receives every result, which gives central control and isolation at the price of extra model calls. In a swarm, specialists hand control directly to each other and the active one keeps talking to the user, which uses fewer calls but spreads control across agents.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A handoff tool returns \u003ccode>Command(goto=\"tech\", graph=Command.PARENT)\u003c/code> and the next model call fails with an error about a missing tool result. What was left out?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A \u003ccode>ToolMessage\u003c/code> answering the handoff tool call (with the matching \u003ccode>tool_call_id\u003c/code>) in the update's messages. Every tool call in the history must have a result, so the handoff must add one.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:durable-execution-for-long-workflows":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Durable execution\u003c/b> means a long-running function survives crashes, deploys and multi-day waits. The engine records the result of every completed step in durable storage. After a failure it runs your orchestration code again from the top, but each already-completed step returns its \u003ci>recorded\u003c/i> result instead of executing again, so the run fast-forwards to the first unfinished step and carries on. This requires the orchestration code to be deterministic and every side effect to live inside a recorded step.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An agent that triages 500 support tickets runs for two hours and makes 3,000 model calls. At ticket 312 the container is replaced during a deploy. Without durability you restart from zero: you pay for 1,800 model calls again, and worse, you may refund a customer or send an email twice. Agents make this common, because they are long-running, call flaky external APIs, and wait for humans.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How replay works\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Your \u003cb>workflow\u003c/b> function contains only orchestration: calls to steps, branches, loops, waits.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Each \u003cb>step\u003c/b> (Temporal calls them \u003ci>activities\u003c/i>) does the real work: a model call, a database write, an API request. The engine records each step's inputs and output in a history (Temporal's \u003ci>Event History\u003c/i>, Restate's \u003ci>journal\u003c/i>, DBOS's Postgres tables, Inngest's state store).\u003c/li>\u003cli>A crash kills the process. The engine (or a peer worker) starts the workflow function again with the same input.\u003c/li>\u003cli>As the code reaches each step, the engine checks the history. Recorded: return the saved result without executing. Not recorded: execute it for real and record the result.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The run continues from the first unrecorded step as if nothing happened.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Run workflow\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Step 1\u003c/b>\u003csmall>recorded, replayed\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Step 2\u003c/b>\u003csmall>recorded, replayed\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Step 3\u003c/b>\u003csmall>crashed here, executes now\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Step 4\u003c/b>\u003csmall>new\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two rules follow. \u003cb>Determinism\u003c/b>: the workflow code must make the same step calls in the same order on replay, so it must not read the clock, generate random numbers or do I/O directly; those go inside steps (engines provide durable timers and seeded random helpers). \u003cb>Idempotent steps\u003c/b>: a step can be retried if it crashes before its result is recorded, so writes to the outside world carry an idempotency key.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Temporal in code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from datetime import timedelta\nfrom temporalio import activity, workflow\n\n@activity.defn\nasync def call_model(prompt: str) -&gt; str: ...           # model API call, retried on failure\n\n@activity.defn\nasync def issue_refund(order_id: str, amount: float) -&gt; str: ...   # uses an idempotency key\n\n@workflow.defn\nclass RefundWorkflow:\n    def __init__(self) -&gt; None:\n        self.approved: bool | None = None\n\n    @workflow.signal\n    def review(self, approved: bool) -&gt; None:           # sent later by the approval UI\n        self.approved = approved\n\n    @workflow.run\n    async def run(self, order_id: str, message: str) -&gt; str:\n        plan = await workflow.execute_activity(\n            call_model, f\"Propose a refund for: {message}\",\n            start_to_close_timeout=timedelta(seconds=60))\n        await workflow.wait_condition(lambda: self.approved is not None,\n                                      timeout=timedelta(days=2))\n        if not self.approved:\n            return \"rejected\"\n        return await workflow.execute_activity(\n            issue_refund, args=[order_id, 80.0],\n            start_to_close_timeout=timedelta(seconds=30))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Activities are retried with exponential backoff by default. The two-day wait costs nothing while it waits: no process is held open, and when the \u003ccode>review\u003c/code> signal arrives a worker replays the history and continues. Integrations exist that run OpenAI Agents SDK and Pydantic AI agents under Temporal, with each model and tool call executed as an activity.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The main engines\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Engine\u003c/th>\u003cth>Model\u003c/th>\u003cth>Where state lives\u003c/th>\u003cth>Human input\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Temporal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Workflows and activities run by your workers against a Temporal service\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Event History in the Temporal cluster or Temporal Cloud\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Signals and updates\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Restate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Handlers whose side effects go through \u003ccode>ctx.run\u003c/code>, recorded in a journal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Restate server (a single Rust binary or cluster)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Awakeables\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DBOS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A library: \u003ccode>@DBOS.workflow()\u003c/code> functions calling \u003ccode>@DBOS.step()\u003c/code> functions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your own Postgres database\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Workflow messages and events\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Inngest\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Event-triggered functions with \u003ccode>step.run\u003c/code>; the service re-invokes your HTTP endpoint and replays memoised step results\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Inngest's state store\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>step.wait_for_event\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Where LangGraph fits\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A LangGraph checkpointer gives a lighter form of durability: state is saved at every super-step and \u003ccode>invoke(None, cfg)\u003c/code> resumes. The differences matter in production:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Granularity\u003c/b>: LangGraph re-runs the whole interrupted node; engines re-run only the unfinished step inside it (LangGraph narrows this gap with \u003ccode>@task\u003c/code> functions, whose results are checkpointed inside a node).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recovery\u003c/b>: LangGraph does not restart crashed runs by itself; an engine's workers pick them up automatically. The LangSmith Agent Server adds this for LangGraph deployments.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Operations\u003c/b>: engines add per-step timeouts, retry policies, durable timers, worker fleets and a UI to inspect and repair stuck runs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Use a checkpointer alone for interactive agents and runs of minutes. Add a durable engine when runs take hours or days, when steps trigger money movement or messages to customers, or when you already operate one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Explain how durable execution survives a crash in the middle of a run.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every completed step's result is recorded in durable storage. After the crash the engine runs the workflow code again from the beginning with the same input; each step that already has a recorded result returns it instantly instead of executing, so no model call or side effect is repeated, and the first step without a record executes for real. This works because the workflow code is deterministic (all clocks, randomness and I/O are inside recorded steps) and steps that might be retried are idempotent.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must workflow code not call \u003ccode>datetime.now()\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>random()\u003c/code> directly?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>On replay the code must make the same step calls in the same order to line up with the recorded history. A different time or random value could send the code down a different branch, so the recorded results no longer match and the engine raises a non-determinism error or produces wrong behaviour.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your LangGraph agent runs for 15 minutes in a chat UI. Do you need Temporal?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Usually not. A Postgres checkpointer plus a small recovery job (or a managed runtime) covers crashes for short interactive runs. Reach for a durable engine when runs last hours or days, steps move money or contact customers, or you need managed retries, timers and worker fleets.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/graphs-beyond-langgraph":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every serious agent framework now has a graph layer, and they share the same ideas: units of work (nodes, executors, steps), explicit edges with conditions, fan-out and fan-in, human input that pauses the run, and checkpoints. The main design difference is \u003cb>how data moves\u003c/b>: LangGraph shares one state object with reducers, while Google ADK and Microsoft Agent Framework pass each node's output to the next node as a message.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Google ADK graph workflows\u003c/h4>\u003cp>ADK 2.0 added a \u003ccode>Workflow\u003c/code> whose \u003ccode>edges\u003c/code> list sequences of nodes. A node can be a function, an agent, a tool or another workflow. A function routes by returning an \u003ccode>Event\u003c/code> with a \u003ccode>route\u003c/code>, and a dictionary maps routes to the next node:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from google.adk import Workflow, Event\n\ndef classify(node_input: str):\n    route = \"refund\" if \"refund\" in node_input.lower() else \"question\"\n    return Event(route=route, output=node_input)\n\ndef handle_refund(node_input: str):\n    return Event(output=f\"refund path: {node_input}\")\n\nroot_agent = Workflow(\n    name=\"support_router\",\n    edges=[\n        (\"START\", classify),\n        (classify, {\"refund\": handle_refund, \"question\": answer_agent}),   # answer_agent is an ADK Agent\n    ],\n)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each node's output is the next node's \u003ccode>node_input\u003c/code>; you do not have to write to shared state (though session state is available). A \u003ccode>JoinNode\u003c/code> waits for parallel branches, and a node that yields \u003ccode>RequestInput(message=..., response_schema=...)\u003c/code> pauses for a human. ADK's older \u003cb>template workflows\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>SequentialAgent\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ParallelAgent\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>LoopAgent\u003c/code>) still work, but recent 2.x releases mark them deprecated in favour of \u003ccode>Workflow\u003c/code>; ADK also offers \u003cb>dynamic workflows\u003c/b> written as ordinary code. The graph API is available in Python, TypeScript and Go.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Microsoft Agent Framework workflows\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Agent Framework (the successor to Semantic Kernel and AutoGen) builds graphs from \u003cb>executors\u003c/b> connected by \u003cb>edges\u003c/b>. Executors receive typed messages and either send messages onward or yield workflow output:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from typing_extensions import Never\nfrom agent_framework import WorkflowBuilder, WorkflowContext, executor\n\n@executor(id=\"classify\")\nasync def classify(text: str, ctx: WorkflowContext[str]) -&gt; None:\n    await ctx.send_message(text)\n\n@executor(id=\"refund\")\nasync def refund(text: str, ctx: WorkflowContext[Never, str]) -&gt; None:\n    await ctx.yield_output(f\"refund path: {text}\")\n\n@executor(id=\"answer\")\nasync def answer(text: str, ctx: WorkflowContext[Never, str]) -&gt; None:\n    await ctx.yield_output(f\"answer path: {text}\")\n\nworkflow = (\n    WorkflowBuilder(start_executor=classify)\n    .add_edge(classify, refund, condition=lambda msg: \"refund\" in msg)\n    .add_edge(classify, answer, condition=lambda msg: \"refund\" not in msg)\n    .build()\n)\nevents = await workflow.run(\"I want a refund for order 42\")\nprint(events.get_outputs())      # ['refund path: I want a refund for order 42']\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>It also offers fan-out and fan-in edge groups (\u003ccode>add_fan_in_edges\u003c/code>), switch-case routing, shared state through \u003ccode>ctx.set_state\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>ctx.get_state\u003c/code>, checkpoints at super-step boundaries, human-in-the-loop requests, sub-workflows, and prebuilt \u003cb>orchestrations\u003c/b> (sequential, concurrent, handoff, group chat and Magentic, where a manager agent coordinates specialists). Python additionally has an experimental functional \u003ccode>@workflow\u003c/code> API that uses plain \u003ccode>if\u003c/code>, loops and \u003ccode>asyncio.gather\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Others you will meet\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pydantic Graph\u003c/b>: each node is a dataclass whose \u003ccode>run\u003c/code> method's return type annotation declares which nodes can follow.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>LlamaIndex Workflows\u003c/b>: event-driven; a step receives an event and returns another event, and the event types define the edges.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mastra\u003c/b> (TypeScript): steps composed with \u003ccode>createWorkflow\u003c/code> using \u003ccode>.then()\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>.branch()\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>.parallel()\u003c/code> and loops, with suspend and resume.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Durable engines\u003c/b> (previous lesson): the workflow is ordinary code rather than a declared graph.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Same ideas, different names\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Concept\u003c/th>\u003cth>LangGraph\u003c/th>\u003cth>Google ADK\u003c/th>\u003cth>Microsoft Agent Framework\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Unit of work\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Node (function, agent, subgraph)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Node (function, agent, tool, workflow)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Executor with typed handlers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Data passing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Shared state with reducers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Output of one node is input to the next, plus session state\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Typed messages along edges, plus shared state\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Routing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Conditional edges or \u003ccode>Command\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Event(route=...)\u003c/code> and a route map\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Edge conditions, switch-case edges\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Parallelism\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Several edges, \u003ccode>Send\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Several edges, \u003ccode>JoinNode\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fan-out and fan-in edge groups\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Human input\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Command(resume=...)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>RequestInput\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Request-info executors\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Persistence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Checkpointers per super-step\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Session services\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Checkpoints per super-step\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Choose by ecosystem more than by features: ADK if you are on Google Cloud and Gemini, Agent Framework if you are on Azure or .NET, LangGraph for a large Python and TypeScript community and provider neutrality. The graph you draw on paper transfers between all of them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the biggest conceptual difference between LangGraph and Microsoft Agent Framework workflows?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>How data moves. LangGraph nodes all read one shared state object and return partial updates merged by reducers. Agent Framework executors receive typed messages from their incoming edges and send typed messages onward (with optional shared state on the side), so edges carry data as well as control.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How does an ADK graph workflow route to different nodes?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A node returns an \u003ccode>Event\u003c/code> with a \u003ccode>route\u003c/code> value, and the workflow's edges include a dictionary that maps each route value to the next node.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name two framework graph features that correspond to LangGraph's \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>ADK nodes that yield \u003ccode>RequestInput\u003c/code> and Agent Framework's request-info mechanism (request-info executors or \u003ccode>ctx.request_info()\u003c/code> in the functional API); both pause the workflow until a response arrives.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/loop-or-graph-a-decision-guide":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use a \u003cb>graph\u003c/b> when you can describe the steps in advance or must guarantee some of them; use an \u003cb>agent loop\u003c/b> when the path depends on what the model discovers; use a \u003cb>hybrid\u003c/b> (a graph whose nodes contain small, bounded agent loops) for most business processes. Start simple and promote repeated paths you see in traces into explicit edges.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Questions that decide it\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Can you list the steps before the run starts?\u003c/b> Yes: graph or plain workflow. No: loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Must some steps always happen, in order?\u003c/b> Identity checks, policy checks, approvals and audit writes belong on edges, not in prompts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Will a run outlive a process?\u003c/b> Waits for humans or runs of hours need checkpoints, and often a durable engine.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Is there parallel work?\u003c/b> Fan-out is natural in a graph and awkward in a single loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Do steps need different models, prompts or tools?\u003c/b> Separate nodes keep each context small and focused.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>How varied are the inputs?\u003c/b> Highly varied, open-ended requests favour a loop; a known set of request types favours routing to fixed paths.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>What does failure cost?\u003c/b> The more expensive a mistake, the more control flow you want in code.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Steps known in advance? ---- no ----&gt; Agent loop with tools, budgets and verification\n        |\n       yes\n        |\nAny judgement inside a step? -- no --&gt; Plain workflow code (no LLM routing needed)\n        |\n       yes\n        |\nGraph with LLM nodes; put a bounded agent loop inside any node whose sub-task is open-ended\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked examples\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Use case\u003c/th>\u003cth>Choice\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Invoice extraction and posting to the ledger\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Graph\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fixed steps, validation and approval above a threshold, needs an audit trail\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Coding agent fixing a failing test\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Loop in a harness\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Path depends on what it finds; verification by tests (Loop engineering module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Support assistant that answers and issues refunds\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hybrid\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Router plus retrieval-and-answer node (small loop inside), refund path with policy check and approval\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Deep research report\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hybrid\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Plan, fan out searches in parallel (each a small loop), synthesise, cite\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Nightly enrichment of 10,000 CRM records\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Workflow plus batch API\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No routing judgement; durability and cost matter most\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What each choice costs\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Graph\u003c/th>\u003cth>Loop\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Predictability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High; paths are enumerable and testable\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Lower; behaviour varies by run\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Handling the unexpected\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Needs a new node or a fallback route\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Adapts on its own\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Upfront work\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Design state, nodes and edges\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Write tools and a prompt\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Evaluation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Per-node tests plus end-to-end evals\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Mostly end-to-end trajectory evals\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost variance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bounded by the paths\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Depends on how many steps the model takes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The path most teams follow\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Prototype with a single agent loop and good tools; it shows you what the task really involves.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Read 50 to 100 traces. Look for paths that repeat (always look up the order, then check the policy) and for steps the model sometimes skips.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Promote those into nodes and edges, keep the model at the decisions that vary, and leave open-ended sub-tasks as small loops inside nodes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add checkpoints, interrupts for risky actions and evals per node.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>The opposite mistake is common too: a 30-node graph with a model call on every edge, built to anticipate every request. If you cannot explain why a node exists from a real trace, delete it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A team wants an \"autonomous agent\" to process expense claims: check receipts, apply the travel policy, get manager approval over 500 EUR, and pay. Loop or graph?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A graph. The steps are known, the policy check and the approval are mandatory and must be enforced in code, the run waits for a manager (so it needs checkpoints), and payment needs an audit trail. Model calls belong inside nodes such as reading the receipt or explaining a policy decision.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When is a hybrid the right answer?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the overall process is known but one or more steps are open-ended, for example \"answer the question from the docs\" inside a support workflow. The graph enforces the process; a small agent loop with its own budget handles the open-ended step.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you find which parts of a working agent loop to turn into graph edges?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Read a sample of real traces, find the sequences that occur on nearly every run and the mandatory steps the model occasionally skips, and make those explicit nodes and edges while keeping the model for decisions that genuinely vary.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:graphs/your-first-graph":"\u003cp>You will build a small refund desk: classify a message, either answer it or draft a refund, pause for a human to approve the refund, then issue it. The graph runs without any API key (a stub stands in for the model), and switches to a real model with two environment variables.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.11 or later\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>pip install langgraph langgraph-checkpoint-sqlite openai\u003c/code> (the \u003ccode>openai\u003c/code> package is only needed for the optional real-model step)\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty folder; all files below go in it\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Create \u003ccode>refund_graph.py\u003c/code> with the state, a model helper and the four nodes:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import os, sqlite3, sys\nfrom typing import Literal, TypedDict\nfrom langgraph.checkpoint.sqlite import SqliteSaver\nfrom langgraph.graph import END, START, StateGraph\nfrom langgraph.types import Command, interrupt\n\nORDERS = {\"42\": {\"item\": \"headphones\", \"price\": 80.0}, \"77\": {\"item\": \"keyboard\", \"price\": 45.0}}\n\ndef llm(prompt: str, stub: str) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Call any OpenAI-compatible model if MODEL is set, else return the stub.\"\"\"\n    if not os.getenv(\"MODEL\"):\n        return stub\n    from openai import OpenAI\n    client = OpenAI(base_url=os.getenv(\"LLM_BASE_URL\"))\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=os.environ[\"MODEL\"], temperature=0,\n                                       messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}])\n    return r.choices[0].message.content.strip()\n\nclass State(TypedDict, total=False):\n    message: str\n    intent: str\n    order_id: str\n    amount: float\n    reply: str\n\ndef classify(state: State) -&gt; dict:\n    msg = state[\"message\"]\n    label = llm(\"Reply with one word, refund or question.\\nMessage: \" + msg,\n                stub=\"refund\" if \"refund\" in msg.lower() else \"question\")\n    order_id = next((w for w in msg.split() if w in ORDERS), \"\")\n    return {\"intent\": \"refund\" if \"refund\" in label.lower() else \"question\", \"order_id\": order_id}\n\ndef answer(state: State) -&gt; dict:\n    return {\"reply\": llm(\"Answer briefly as a support agent: \" + state[\"message\"],\n                         stub=\"(stub) See the help centre article on this topic.\")}\n\ndef draft_refund(state: State) -&gt; dict:\n    order = ORDERS.get(state.get(\"order_id\", \"\"))\n    if order is None:\n        return {\"reply\": \"Which order number is this about?\"}\n    return {\"amount\": order[\"price\"]}\n\ndef approve_and_issue(state: State) -&gt; dict:\n    decision = interrupt({\"order_id\": state[\"order_id\"], \"amount\": state[\"amount\"],\n                          \"question\": \"Approve this refund? Reply approve or reject.\"})\n    if decision != \"approve\":\n        return {\"reply\": \"A colleague reviewed your request and could not approve the refund.\"}\n    # The side effect runs after the interrupt, so it runs once, after approval.\n    print(f\"[payments] refunding {state['amount']} for order {state['order_id']}\")\n    return {\"reply\": f\"Refund of {state['amount']:.2f} issued for order {state['order_id']}.\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Append the routing functions, the graph wiring with a SQLite checkpointer, and a tiny command-line interface:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def route_intent(state: State) -&gt; Literal[\"answer\", \"draft_refund\"]:\n    return \"draft_refund\" if state[\"intent\"] == \"refund\" else \"answer\"\n\ndef route_draft(state: State) -&gt; Literal[\"approve_and_issue\", \"__end__\"]:\n    return \"approve_and_issue\" if \"amount\" in state else END\n\nbuilder = StateGraph(State)\nbuilder.add_node(\"classify\", classify)\nbuilder.add_node(\"answer\", answer)\nbuilder.add_node(\"draft_refund\", draft_refund)\nbuilder.add_node(\"approve_and_issue\", approve_and_issue)\nbuilder.add_edge(START, \"classify\")\nbuilder.add_conditional_edges(\"classify\", route_intent)\nbuilder.add_conditional_edges(\"draft_refund\", route_draft)\nbuilder.add_edge(\"answer\", END)\nbuilder.add_edge(\"approve_and_issue\", END)\n\nconn = sqlite3.connect(\"refunds.db\", check_same_thread=False)\ngraph = builder.compile(checkpointer=SqliteSaver(conn))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    cmd, thread = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2] if len(sys.argv) &gt; 2 else \"-\"\n    cfg = {\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": thread}}\n    if cmd == \"start\":\n        out = graph.invoke({\"message\": sys.argv[3]}, cfg)\n    elif cmd == \"resume\":\n        out = graph.invoke(Command(resume=sys.argv[3]), cfg)\n    elif cmd == \"history\":\n        for snap in graph.get_state_history(cfg):\n            print(snap.metadata[\"step\"], snap.next, sorted(snap.values))\n        sys.exit()\n    elif cmd == \"draw\":\n        print(graph.get_graph().draw_mermaid())\n        sys.exit()\n    if \"__interrupt__\" in out:\n        print(\"PAUSED:\", out[\"__interrupt__\"][0].value)\n    else:\n        print(\"REPLY:\", out[\"reply\"])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Run a question through the graph:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>python refund_graph.py start t1 \"How do I pair my headphones?\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>Start a refund on a new thread. The run pauses at the interrupt and the process exits:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>python refund_graph.py start t2 \"I want a refund for order 42 please\"\npython refund_graph.py history t2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"5\">\u003cli>Approve it from a fresh process. This is the point of the checkpoint: the paused state was loaded from \u003ccode>refunds.db\u003c/code>, not from memory.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>python refund_graph.py resume t2 approve\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"6\">\u003cli>Exercise the other paths: start thread \u003ccode>t3\u003c/code> with \"refund order 77\" and resume it with \u003ccode>reject\u003c/code>; start thread \u003ccode>t4\u003c/code> with \"refund my thing\" (no order number).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Prove that the interrupted node re-runs on resume: add \u003ccode>print(\"approve_and_issue starts\")\u003c/code> as the first line of \u003ccode>approve_and_issue\u003c/code>, start thread \u003ccode>t5\u003c/code> with a refund for order 42, then resume it. The line prints in both processes, but the \u003ccode>[payments]\u003c/code> line prints only once, because it sits after \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Print the graph with \u003ccode>python refund_graph.py draw\u003c/code> and paste the output into any Mermaid viewer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Optional, real model: set \u003ccode>MODEL=&lt;model&gt;\u003c/code> and, for a local or non-OpenAI server, \u003ccode>LLM_BASE_URL\u003c/code> (for example \u003ccode>http://localhost:11434/v1\u003c/code> for Ollama) plus \u003ccode>OPENAI_API_KEY\u003c/code> (any value for Ollama). Rerun steps 3 to 6 and compare the routing.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>REPLY: (stub) See the help centre article on this topic.\nPAUSED: {'order_id': '42', 'amount': 80.0, 'question': 'Approve this refund? Reply approve or reject.'}\n2 ('approve_and_issue',) ['amount', 'intent', 'message', 'order_id']\n1 ('draft_refund',) ['intent', 'message', 'order_id']\n0 ('classify',) ['message']\n-1 ('__start__',) []\n[payments] refunding 80.0 for order 42\nREPLY: Refund of 80.00 issued for order 42.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Thread \u003ccode>t3\u003c/code> ends with the rejection reply and no payment line; thread \u003ccode>t4\u003c/code> ends at \u003ccode>draft_refund\u003c/code> with \"Which order number is this about?\" and never reaches the approval node.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why did the approval survive the process exiting between steps 4 and 5?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The graph was compiled with a SQLite checkpointer, so when \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> paused the run the state and the pending interrupt were written to \u003ccode>refunds.db\u003c/code> under thread \u003ccode>t2\u003c/code>. The second process compiled the same graph against the same database and resumed that thread with \u003ccode>Command(resume=\"approve\")\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What would go wrong if the \u003ccode>[payments]\u003c/code> print (standing in for a real payment call) were moved above \u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It would run once when the node first executes and pauses, and again when the node re-runs on resume, so the customer would be refunded twice (or once even when the reviewer rejects).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:graphs/atlas-m9-atlas-as-a-graph":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Starting point: Atlas after M8 (grounded answers with citations over your corpus, ideally with the retrieval upgrade from the Advanced retrieval build), plus the typed tools from M4 and the memory from M6. Today Atlas is probably one function or a single agent loop. This milestone makes its control flow explicit. Every message is routed; questions go through retrieval and a grounded answer; actions (create a ticket, look up a record, run a query) go through typed planning, a policy check and human approval; and every conversation is a checkpointed thread, so it resumes after a restart and a pending approval can wait for hours.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A LangGraph \u003ccode>StateGraph\u003c/code> with at least these nodes: \u003ccode>route\u003c/code> (rules first, then a small model), \u003ccode>retrieve\u003c/code> (your M7 or M8 hybrid search), \u003ccode>answer\u003c/code> (grounded, with citations), \u003ccode>check_citations\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>plan_action\u003c/code> (choose a tool and fill its M4 Pydantic arguments), \u003ccode>approve\u003c/code> (interrupt), \u003ccode>execute_action\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>clarify\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>respond\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A state schema with \u003ccode>messages\u003c/code> (using \u003ccode>add_messages\u003c/code>) and typed keys for intent, retrieved document ids, the proposed action, the approval decision and running cost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One bounded cycle: if \u003ccode>check_citations\u003c/code> fails, retrieve again with a reformulated query at most once, then answer with a clear caveat.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A durable checkpointer (SQLite for development, Postgres if you can) and one \u003ccode>thread_id\u003c/code> per conversation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An approval payload a human can understand (\"Create ticket in project OPS titled ... with priority P2\"), resumable with approve, edit or reject. Read-only actions may skip approval, decided by a policy function in code, not by the model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Side effects only in \u003ccode>execute_action\u003c/code>, after approval, with an idempotency key derived from the thread id and the action, and an audit-log row per decision.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A small interface: CLI commands or HTTP endpoints for \u003ccode>chat(thread_id, message)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pending_approvals()\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>decide(thread_id, decision)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>state(thread_id)\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Per-node logging of tokens, cost and latency, reusing your M1 and M2 code.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Draw the graph and fill in a state table (key, type, reducer, writer). Mark which nodes call a model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implement the state and nodes as thin adapters over your existing Atlas modules; unit-test each node with hand-written state dictionaries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Wire the edges and the router. Label 50 real or realistic messages (question, action, unclear) and report the router's confusion matrix; fix the worst confusion.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add the checkpointer and thread ids. Test a five-turn conversation that refers back to earlier turns.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build the action path: planning with typed arguments, the policy function, the interrupt, the decision endpoint, the idempotency key and the audit log.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add the citation-check cycle with its counter and set a per-call \u003ccode>recursion_limit\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Crash test: make \u003ccode>execute_action\u003c/code> raise once after approval, then resume the thread with \u003ccode>invoke(None, cfg)\u003c/code> and confirm the action runs exactly once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Regression check: run your M8 RAG eval through the graph and confirm quality is within noise; produce a table of median latency and cost per path.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write \u003ccode>ARCHITECTURE.md\u003c/code> with the Mermaid diagram from \u003ccode>get_graph().draw_mermaid()\u003c/code> and a paragraph on each design decision.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Question, action and unclear messages take the correct paths, and the router's accuracy on your 50 labelled messages is reported in the README.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An action request pauses, appears in \u003ccode>pending_approvals()\u003c/code>, and can be approved from a different process hours later; a test proves that repeating the decision or resuming twice cannot execute the action twice.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Killing or failing the process mid-run and resuming completes the run without repeating completed steps.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The RAG eval score is within noise of M8, and the README shows latency and cost per path.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The graph diagram and architecture notes are committed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Add a debug command that forks any thread from any checkpoint with edited state (time travel) and reruns it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Fan out retrieval to two indexes in parallel with a reducer, and measure the latency change.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add an expiry job that rejects approvals older than 24 hours and notifies the user.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Port the action path to Temporal or DBOS and compare the code and the failure behaviour.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stream answer tokens to your UI with \u003ccode>graph.stream_events(..., version=\"v3\")\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keep Atlas's core modules framework-free; nodes should be a few lines that call them. You will thank yourself in the Frameworks module build.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Put the model client, database handles and the tool registry in runtime context, not in state.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Store pending approvals (thread id, interrupt id, payload, created time) in a small table when the graph pauses, so a reviewer UI can list them without scanning checkpoints.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
